 [["`", -0.00017167611804325134], ["2", -11.638835906982422]]}, {"t": " driver", "lp": -0.0014579190174117684, "top": [[" driver", -0.0014579190174117684], [" kernel", -7.641415596008301]]}, {"t": ",", "lp": -0.3416958153247833, "top": [[",", -0.3416958153247833], [" source", -2.1618194580078125]]}, {"t": " as", "lp": -0.7635127902030945, "top": [[" as", -0.7635127902030945], [" and", -0.9203845858573914]]}, {"t": " these", "lp": -0.3474642336368561, "top": [[" these", -0.3474642336368561], [" this", -1.3947473764419556]]}, {"t": " details", "lp": -0.6337084174156189, "top": [[" details", -0.6337084174156189], [" can", -1.6034425497055054]]}, {"t": " can", "lp": -0.35917040705680847, "top": [[" can", -0.35917040705680847], [" have", -2.1416335105895996]]}, {"t": " shift", "lp": -0.37423691153526306, "top": [[" shift", -0.37423691153526306], [" vary", -1.8103045225143433]]}, {"t": " between", "lp": -0.03304555267095566, "top": [[" between", -0.03304555267095566], [" across", -4.054379463195801]]}, {"t": " driver", "lp": -0.20288188755512238, "top": [[" driver", -0.20288188755512238], [" kernel", -1.700922966003418]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are distributed across the GPU's compute engines. This is relevant because the Arc A770 has multiple CCS engines, and how you partition them affects workload scheduling.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**ccs_mode 1** \u2014 *Balanced/Default*\n- Distributes CCS contexts across all available compute engines in a balanced manner\n- This is generally the safe default that works well for most workloads\n- Good for mixed workloads where you want even distribution\n\n**ccs_mode 2** \u2014 *Partitioned*\n- Partitions the compute engines into groups\n- Useful when you want to isolate workloads or have specific scheduling requirements\n- Can help avoid contention between different types of jobs\n\n**ccs_mode 4** \u2014 *Per-engine / dedicated*\n- Assigns contexts to specific engines more rigidly\n- Typically used when you need **deterministic scheduling** or want to **pin workloads to specific engines**\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n| Mode | Use Case |\n|------|----------|\n| 1 | Default, general-purpose, mixed workloads |\n| 2 | Workload isolation, reducing cross-engine contention |\n| 4 | Deterministic scheduling, pinned/dedicated engines |\n\n## Practical Considerations\n\nFor most users, **ccs_mode 1** is the right choice. The differences are subtle and mostly matter for:\n\n- **Benchmarking** \u2014 where you want to isolate variables\n- **Specific compute workloads** \u2014 that benefit from dedicated engines\n- **Debugging scheduling issues**\n\n---\n\n**A note of honesty:** I'm not fully certain about the exact semantics of each numeric mode in the current `xe` driver, as these details can shift between driver"}